notify_start: honour SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE

read_passphrase() uses the askpass when SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE is "force",
or "prefer" with a display, even when there is a terminal. notify_start()
ignores the variable and writes to the terminal whenever stderr is a tty.
So with SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=prefer, ssh asks a FIDO key's PIN through the
askpass and then the "Confirm user presence" request goes to a terminal
the user isn't looking at.

Make notify_start() treat SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE the same way as
read_passphrase(): "force" always uses the askpass, "prefer" uses it when
DISPLAY or WAYLAND_DISPLAY is set, and anything else keeps the current
behaviour.

--- a/readpass.c
+++ b/readpass.c
@@ -249,6 +249,19 @@
 
 	if (fflush(NULL) != 0)
 		error_f("fflush: %s", strerror(errno));
+	/*
+	 * Honour SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE as read_passphrase() does, so a PIN
+	 * asked through the askpass is followed by an askpass notification
+	 * rather than a message on the terminal.
+	 */
+	if (!force_askpass && (s = getenv(SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE_ENV)) != NULL) {
+		if (strcasecmp(s, "force") == 0)
+			force_askpass = 1;
+		else if (strcasecmp(s, "prefer") == 0)
+			force_askpass =
+			    ((s = getenv("DISPLAY")) != NULL && *s != '\0') ||
+			    ((s = getenv("WAYLAND_DISPLAY")) != NULL && *s != '\0');
+	}
 	if (!force_askpass && isatty(STDERR_FILENO)) {
 		writemsg(prompt);
 		goto out_ctx;
